# Lab 01 – Configure Hostname Resolution and Test Network Connectivity

## Objective

Configure hostname resolution between two RHEL servers by adding host-to-IP mappings to the `/etc/hosts` file and verify network connectivity between the systems using their assigned IP addresses and hostnames.

This lab also reinforced systematic network troubleshooting by working through the network stack from interface and IP configuration down to Layer 2 connectivity and ARP.

## Environment

- Host: MacBook Pro
- Hypervisor: VirtualBox
- Guest OS: Red Hat Enterprise Linux
- Servers: Server10 and Server30
- Network interfaces: enp0s8 and enp0s9

## Network Configuration

### Server10

enp0s8:
192.168.68.2

enp0s9:
172.10.10.110/24

### Server30

enp0s8:
192.168.68.17

enp0s9:
172.10.10.120/24

The enp0s9 interfaces were used for the dedicated network between Server10 and Server30.

Both systems were configured on the 172.10.10.0/24 subnet.

## VirtualBox Network Configuration

A second virtual network adapter was added to both Server10 and Server30.

The second adapter appeared in RHEL as enp0s9.

Both VirtualBox Adapter 2 configurations were verified as:

Enabled: Yes
Attached to: Internal Network
Name: intnet

Using the same Internal Network name allowed both virtual machines to communicate through the same isolated Layer 2 network.

## Commands Used

    ip addr
    ip addr show enp0s9
    ip route
    ip route get 172.10.10.120
    ip route get 172.10.10.110
    ip link show enp0s9
